          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life and Education</strong><br>\nGeorgia O'Keeffe was born in 1887 in Sun Prairie, Wisconsin. She decided to become an artist at a young age and studied at the Art Institute of Chicago and the Art Students League in New York. O'Keeffe's early work was heavily influenced by her art school training but she soon developed her own style.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Style and Artistic Themes</strong><br>\nO'Keeffe's distinctive personal style comprised of large-scale, close-up paintings of natural forms like flowers, bones, and landscapes. Her work often explored the intricate details of her subject matter, creating abstracted compositions from these natural elements.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Relationship with Alfred Stieglitz</strong><br>\nPhotographer Alfred Stieglitz played a crucial role in O'Keeffe's artistic career, promoting her work and marrying her in 1924. Their relationship was a partnership of mutual creative influence and shared a passion for modern art.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Life in New Mexico</strong><br>\nO'Keeffe's relocation to New Mexico marked a profound shift in her work. The vast landscapes, indigenous art, and unique architecture of the region deeply influenced her palette and subject matter, leading to some of her most famous landscape paintings.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Iconic Works</strong><br>\nO'Keeffe is perhaps best known for her paintings of flowers, which are often interpreted as reflecting femininity and nature's beauty. Her New Mexico works, like \"Black Cross, New Mexico\" and \"Cow's Skull: Red, White, and Blue,\" show her fascination with the desert and its corresponding symbols.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Innovations in American Art</strong><br>\nO'Keeffe is recognized for her contributions to American modernism. Her paintings defied the norms of her time by portraying a personal interpretation of what she saw and experienced, making her one of the most innovative artists of the 20th century.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
